  }
  \subsection{Returns}{
    A list of MAIDR interval points
  }
}

\if{html}{\out{<hr>}}
\if{html}{\out{<a id="method-Ggplot2ErrorbarLayerProcessor-resolve_estimates"></a>}}
\if{latex}{\out{\hypertarget{method-Ggplot2ErrorbarLayerProcessor-resolve_estimates}{}}}
\subsection{\code{Ggplot2ErrorbarLayerProcessor$resolve_estimates()}}{
  Resolve the estimate each interval is centred on.

The estimate aesthetic is \strong{optional} on these geoms, and leaving it
out is idiomatic rather than exotic:
\code{geom_errorbar(aes(x, ymin, ymax))} layered over a \code{geom_col()} is the
standard way to draw a bar chart with error bars, and it builds with no
\code{y} column at all. \code{geom_linerange()} is the same. Requiring the column
dropped every such layer silently -- no interval, no estimate, no error.

When it is absent the chart genuinely draws no estimate, only a span, so
the centre of that span is used. That is a property of the drawn bar
rather than a claim about an unobserved estimate, and it is what keeps
the bounds -- which are the real data here -- reachable at all. It is
NOT the mean for an asymmetric interval, and nothing here pretends it
is: a layer that carries \code{y} always uses the value the chart drew.
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{Ggplot2ErrorbarLayerProcessor$resolve_estimates(
  layer_data,
  value_col,
  min_col,
  max_col
)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Arguments}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
    \describe{
      \item{\code{layer_data}}{This layer's computed rows}
      \item{\code{value_col}}{The estimate column for this orientation}
      \item{\code{min_col}}{The lower bound column for this orientation}
      \item{\code{max_col}}{The upper bound column for this orientation}
    }
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Returns}{
    A numeric vector of estimates, or NULL when neither the estimate
nor a pair of bounds is present
  }
}

\if{html}{\out{<hr>}}
\if{html}{\out{<a id="method-Ggplot2ErrorbarLayerProcessor-resolve_category_labels"></a>}}
\if{latex}{\out{\hypertarget{method-Ggplot2ErrorbarLayerProcessor-resolve_category_labels}{}}}
\subsection{\code{Ggplot2ErrorbarLayerProcessor$resolve_category_labels()}}{
  Recover the names behind a discrete category axis.

ggplot2 maps a discrete axis onto integer positions before it computes
the layer, so the built data carries \verb{1, 2, 3} where the chart draws
\verb{control, high dose, low dose}. Announcing the positions would name
something the reader cannot find anywhere on the chart -- and the
positions are assigned in the scale's order, not the data's, so they do
not even read as row numbers.

The labels come from the panel's scale rather than from the data frame,
which is what makes the position an index into them.
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
    \preformatted{Ggplot2ErrorbarLayerProcessor$resolve_category_labels(
  built,
  layer_data,
  category_col,
  panel_id = NULL
)}
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Arguments}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="arguments">}}
    \describe{
      \item{\code{built}}{Built plot data}
      \item{\code{layer_data}}{This layer's computed rows}
      \item{\code{category_col}}{Which built column carries the category}
      \item{\code{panel_id}}{Panel ID for faceted plots (optional)}
    }
    \if{html}{\out{</div>}}
  }
  \subsection{Returns}{
    A list of labels -- strings for a discrete axis, numbers for a
continuous one
  }
}

